{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And the sons of Aaron the priest shall put fire upon the altar.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in tractate Yoma, chapter 'They brought him out,' that the sons of Aaron died only because they ruled a law in the presence of Moses their master. What is the exposition? ״ונתנו בני אהרן הכהנים אש על המזבח״ — they said: even though fire descends from heaven, there is a commandment to bring from an ordinary source." "<b>And Saadia the Gaon wrote</b> that in this matter Nadav and Avihu erred, for they thought that the word ונתנו means that they should place fire from outside upon the altar, but it is not so; rather, the word ונתנו is like the expression ובערו ('and they kindled'), for we find the expression of נתינה ('putting') whose meaning is kindling, and he brought proof from what Hezekiah said (Isaiah 37:19): ״ונתון את אלהיהם באש״. And he said that from this one may demonstrate how much a person must be careful with the precise meaning of words in the language of our Torah, for even Nadav and Avihu, than whom there was none in all Israel greater after Moses, erred in the meaning of the expression ונתנו, and their error was a cause for them to bring alien fire from outside, and they were punished with the penalty of death — for they said that when Moses said by mouth of the Almighty, ״ונתנו בני אהרן הכהן אש״, the meaning is that they should place upon the altar fire from outside, and they did not take heed in this matter to ask Moses, and this punishment reached them. Therefore the Sages of the Talmud were careful to be precise in the language of their master so as not to alter the intention in any way, and from here they said: a person is obligated to speak in the language of his master. And because of the exceeding greatness of the care that a person must exercise in the words of the Torah in the words of the Holy Language, Scripture called one who alters the intent of words in our holy language one who perverts the words of the living Elohim — this is what Jeremiah the prophet, peace be upon him, said (Jeremiah 23:33–36): ״And when this people, or the prophet, or a priest shall ask you, saying, What is the burden of HaShem? you shall say to them, What burden? And I will forsake you, says HaShem. And as for the prophet, and the priest, and the people, that shall say, The burden of HaShem, I will visit punishment upon that man and upon his house. Thus shall you say every one to his neighbor, and every one to his brother, What has HaShem answered? and what has HaShem spoken? And the burden of HaShem shall you mention no more, for every man's word shall be his burden.״ That is, the meaning of 'the burden' is not for you except for the man, the prophet, who hears His word; and if you are cunning about the interpretation of 'the burden' other than according to what the prophet commands, and you alter the intent of his words, you pervert the words of the living Elohim — this is what it says (ibid.): ״והפכתם את דברי אלהים חיים ה' צבאות אלהינו״. Scripture describes HaShem, may He be exalted, with these three epithets, to make clear that in doing this they come against the patriarchs, and against the prophets, and against the Holy One, blessed be He, who revealed Himself at Mount Sinai. He said 'Elohim חיים' on account of Mount Sinai, as it is said (Deuteronomy 4:33): ״השמע עם קול אלהים חיים״; and He said 'HaShem צבאות' on account of the prophets, as it is written (Zechariah 7:12): ״אשר שלח ה' צבאות ברוחו ביד הנביאים הראשונים״; and He said 'Eloheinu' on account of the patriarchs, as it is said (Joshua 24:17): ״כי ה' אלהינו הוא המעלה אותנו ואת אבותינו״ and so on. From here is an explicit proof of how much a person is obligated to be careful in the interpretation of the holy books, in verses and in words — end of quote. And he, of blessed memory, explained the word ונתנו in the Arabic language 'ישעלו\"ן' (they kindle), like the expression ובערו, in the manner of what is written (Leviticus 6:5): ״ובער עליה הכהן עצים״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :register {:flags {1 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:22:40.272187872Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}